{"name":"Brood","hex":"#69605a","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"brood","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The young of certain animals, especially a group of young birds or fowl hatched at one time by the same mother.","The young of any egg-laying creature, especially if produced at the same time."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Kept or reared for breeding."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brood"}],"entries":[{"title":"Offspring","blocks":[{"text":"In biology, offspring are the young creation of living organisms, produced either by sexual or asexual reproduction. Collective offspring may be known as a brood or progeny. This can refer to a set of simultaneous offspring, such as the chicks hatched from one clutch of eggs, or to all offspring produced over time, as with the honeybee. Offspring can occur after mating, artificial insemination, or as a result of cloning."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Hybridogenesis_in_water_frogs_gametes.svg/330px-Hybridogenesis_in_water_frogs_gametes.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Offspring","wikidata":"Q239526"},{"title":"Broodiness","blocks":[{"text":"Broodiness is the action or behavioral tendency to sit on a clutch of eggs to incubate them, often requiring the non-expression of many other behaviors including feeding and drinking. Being broody has been defined as \"Being in a state of readiness to brood eggs that is characterized by cessation of laying and by marked changes in behavior and physiology\". Broodiness is usually associated with female birds, although males of some bird species become broody and some non-avian animals also show broodiness."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Incubating_white_tern.jpg/330px-Incubating_white_tern.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broodiness","wikidata":"Q4201371"},{"title":"Brood (comics)","blocks":[{"text":"The Brood are a fictional race of insectoid, parasitic, extraterrestrial beings appearing in American comic books published by Marvel Comics, especially Uncanny X-Men. Created by writer Chris Claremont and artist Dave Cockrum, they first appeared in The Uncanny X-Men #155."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/8/8a/Brood_vs_Ms_Marvel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brood_(comics)","wikidata":"Q897901"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Frog_in_frogspawn.jpg/500px-Frog_in_frogspawn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Frog_in_frogspawn.jpg/1280px-Frog_in_frogspawn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Frog_in_frogspawn.jpg/500px-Frog_in_frogspawn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A frog in frogspawn","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFrog_in_frogspawn.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/A_brooding_scene_2.JPG/500px-A_brooding_scene_2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/A_brooding_scene_2.JPG/1280px-A_brooding_scene_2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/A_brooding_scene_2.JPG/500px-A_brooding_scene_2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A brooding domestic hen.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AA_brooding_scene_2.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/co